# Custom Lovelace Card Guidelines

Guidelines for building custom Home Assistant Lovelace cards that work across all target devices:

- **Desktop**: Chrome/Firefox/Edge on Windows/Mac/Linux
- **iOS**: Home Assistant Companion App (iPhone, iPad)
- **Android**: Home Assistant Companion App, UniFi Connect Display (wall-mounted touchscreen)

## 1) Communication: relay script pattern only

Cards **must not** use `fire_event`, `callApi("POST", "events/...")`, or `connection.sendMessagePromise({ type: "fire_event" })`. These require **admin privileges** and fail silently on non-admin device accounts.

Use `hass.callService()` exclusively — it works for all authenticated users:

```javascript
_callRelay(command, data) {
  if (!this._hass) return;
  this._hass.callService("script", "<app>_relay", {
    command,
    payload: JSON.stringify(data || {}),
  }).catch((err) => {
    console.warn("<card>: relay failed", command, err);
  });
}
```

See `appdaemon-architecture.mdc` §3 for the full relay script pattern.

## 2) Touch + click event delegation

Use a single delegated event system on the shadow root. Mark interactive elements with `data-action` attributes and route through a central `_dispatchAction(action, el)` handler.

### The problem

Touch devices fire `touchstart` → `touchend` → `click` in sequence. Without deduplication, actions fire twice. Android webviews (especially UniFi Connect) are stricter about `preventDefault()` blocking native form behavior than iOS or desktop.

### Required pattern

```javascript
_ensureDelegatedListeners() {
  if (this._delegatedBound) return;
  this._delegatedBound = true;

  const root = this.shadowRoot;
  let touchActive = false;
  let touchCancelled = false;

  // Cancel touch on scroll/move
  ["touchcancel", "touchmove", "scroll"].forEach((evt) => {
    root.addEventListener(evt, () => { touchCancelled = true; }, { passive: true });
  });

  root.addEventListener("touchstart", () => {
    touchActive = true;
    touchCancelled = false;
  }, { passive: true });

  root.addEventListener("touchend", (e) => {
    const el = findActionTarget(e);
    if (touchCancelled || !el) { touchActive = false; return; }

    // CRITICAL: never preventDefault on native form elements — Android
    // webviews will not open keyboards, dropdowns, or date pickers.
    const tag = el.tagName?.toLowerCase();
    const nativeEl = tag === "input" || tag === "select" || tag === "textarea";
    if (!nativeEl && e.cancelable) e.preventDefault();

    dispatchAction(el);
    setTimeout(() => { touchActive = false; }, 400);
  });

  // Desktop click — skip if touch just handled it
  root.addEventListener("click", (e) => {
    if (touchActive) return;
    const el = findActionTarget(e);
    if (el) dispatchAction(el);
  });
}
```

### Key rules

| Rule | Why |
|---|---|
| **Never** `preventDefault()` on `<input>`, `<select>`, `<textarea>` touchend | Android webviews won't open keyboard or dropdown |
| Prefer avoiding `preventDefault()` on `touchend` entirely when click dedupe is already handled by a `touchActive` guard | Android wall-display webviews can treat it as scroll-hostile even for non-form controls |
| Use `{ passive: true }` for `touchstart`, `touchmove`, `touchcancel`, `scroll` | Required by modern browsers; improves scroll performance |
| Gate click handler with `if (touchActive) return` | Prevents double-fire on touch devices |
| Use 300–400ms timeout to clear `touchActive` | Covers the touch→click delay on all platforms |
| Use `e.composedPath()` to find `data-action` target | Works correctly inside shadow DOM |

### Android wall-display note

On Android wall tablets / embedded webviews, global shadow-root touch listeners are more likely to interfere with native vertical scrolling than on phones.

- Prefer tap-to-act for dense editor surfaces instead of touch-drag interactions.
- If mouse drag behavior is needed, keep it mouse-only unless touch drag is essential.
- Set `touch-action: pan-y` on `:host`, primary card containers, and large editor wrappers so the browser can keep vertical scroll ownership.
- Do not assume behavior seen in the Android phone companion app matches the wall tablet webview.

## 3) Form inputs must not lose focus on re-render

When the card re-renders (e.g., after `_markDirty()`), the entire shadow DOM is replaced, stealing focus from any active input field. This makes typing impossible — each keystroke triggers a re-render that deselects the field.

### Fix

Before re-rendering, check if the active element is an input. If so, skip the render or defer it:

```javascript
_render() {
  const active = this.shadowRoot?.activeElement;
  if (active && (active.tagName === "INPUT" || active.tagName === "TEXTAREA")) {
    // Don't re-render while user is typing — the save button is already enabled
    return;
  }
  // ... actual render logic
}
```

For date inputs (`<input type="date">`), also skip re-render since the date picker counts as an active input on mobile.

## 4) Structural changes require explicit save

Actions that modify the card's data model (add, remove, reorder items) should call `_markDirty()` and re-render — **not** auto-save. The user must click "Save" to persist changes. This allows discarding unintentional actions (e.g., accidental delete).

Auto-save is only appropriate for actions that are explicitly confirmatory (the Save button itself).

## 5) Static card JS cache busting

Custom cards served from `/local/` or `/config/www/` are aggressively cached by browsers and HA companion apps. After updating a card's JS file on the HA server:

1. Update the Lovelace resource URL's query parameter: `?v=N` → `?v=N+1`
2. Users must hard-refresh (Ctrl+Shift+R on desktop, pull-to-refresh on mobile)

Use the MCP `ha_config_set_dashboard_resource` tool to bump the version:

```
resource_id: "<id from ha_config_list_dashboard_resources>"
url: "/local/path/to/card.js?v=<N+1>"
```

## 6) Dynamic media URLs must be stable across state updates

For cards that display images from HA sensors, do **not** append `?t=${Date.now()}` or similar per-render / per-publish cache-busters to image URLs.

### The problem

If the backend republishes the same image file with a fresh timestamp query param on every state update, companion apps treat it as a brand-new image and re-fetch it. On iOS this causes:

- black frames while the image reloads
- slow repaints after taps/swipes
- one client showing the correct next image while another still displays the old bitmap

This was the root cause of the wall-display notification/photo-frame iOS regressions.

### Required pattern

- Card JS files: use manual Lovelace resource `?v=N` cache busting as described in §5.
- Media/image URLs from AppDaemon sensors: keep them stable unless the underlying file actually changes.
- Prefer a file-derived version such as mtime or generation ID:

```python
def _versioned_media_url(local_url: str, file_path: str | None) -> str:
    if not local_url or not file_path:
        return local_url
    try:
        version = int(os.path.getmtime(file_path))
    except OSError:
        return local_url
    return f"{local_url}?v={version}"
```

### Rules

| Rule | Why |
|---|---|
| Never use `time.time()` / `Date.now()` as a media URL query param on every publish | Forces pointless re-downloads |
| Only change media URL when file content changes | Lets browsers keep decoded images alive |
| Keep card JS cache busting separate from media URL versioning | They solve different caching problems |

## 7) Fixed-size media cards

Cards that show photos, generated images, or camera-like stills should reserve a stable visual footprint. Do not let the card height depend on the currently displayed image's aspect ratio.

### Required pattern

- Use a fixed `aspect-ratio` or explicit `height` on the media container.
- Display the main image with `object-fit: contain`.
- Use a neutral or blurred backdrop behind the contained image so landscape and portrait assets both look intentional.

```css
.frame-stage {
  position: relative;
  width: 100%;
  aspect-ratio: 3 / 4; /* or configurable */
  overflow: hidden;
}

.frame-image {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
  object-fit: contain;
}
```

### Rules

| Rule | Why |
|---|---|
| Do not use markdown `<img>` cards for rotating photo/media layouts | Card height shifts with image aspect ratio |
| Prefer `aspect_ratio` config, allow `height` override | Makes dashboard sizing intentional |
| Use backdrop filler instead of stretching/cropping | Keeps photo-frame look without layout jump |

## 8) iOS WebView image rendering is special

The HA iOS app can keep painting an old bitmap even after state and text update correctly. This is especially likely when animating `<img>` elements or when reusing the same DOM node during rapid source changes.

### Safer patterns

- Prefer discrete transitions (cross-fade on release) over live drag transforms of `<img>` content.
- If the visible image refuses to repaint, replace the current `<img>` node instead of only mutating `src`.
- Keep off-screen image layers mounted if they are part of an animation.
- Avoid depending on a container-width measurement during first paint for layout; use CSS positioning for base slide placement.

### Swipe guidance

For swipe navigation:

- Prefer "gesture on release triggers next/previous" over continuous dragging unless live drag is truly necessary.
- If continuous drag is used, move slide layers, not a shared strip, and test on iOS specifically.
- Do not block all touch interaction behind preload gates; if media readiness matters, degrade gracefully.

## 9) Manual navigation often has multiple code paths

If a card supports both buttons and swipe navigation, assume they do **not** share the same execution path unless you verify it directly.

### The problem

A common regression is to implement a behavioral change such as "manual navigation should pause autoplay" in the button handler only, while swipe completion still calls the backend command directly. That makes one interaction path correct and the other inconsistent.

There is also a state-ordering issue: if a swipe sends `pause` before `next`/`previous`, the card can briefly re-render the old item in a paused state before the index update arrives.

### Required pattern

- Route all manual navigation through one helper when possible.
- If swipe completion must bypass the normal button dispatcher, explicitly apply the same side effects there too.
- When a swipe should both navigate and pause, prefer sending the navigation command first and the pause command second.

```javascript
_pauseForManualNav() {
  if (this._isPaused()) return;
  this._callRelay("toggle_pause");
}

_dispatchAction(action) {
  if (action === "next" || action === "previous") {
    this._pauseForManualNav();
  }
  this._callRelay(action);
}

_finishSwipe(direction) {
  const cmd = direction === "left" ? "next" : "previous";
  this._callRelay(cmd);
  this._pauseForManualNav();
}
```

### Rules

| Rule | Why |
|---|---|
| Test button nav and swipe nav separately after any behavior change | They often bypass each other |
| Do not assume swipe uses the same dispatcher as buttons | Many cards call relay commands directly in swipe completion |
| For "navigate + pause", send nav first and pause second | Prevents a stale paused render of the old item |

## 10) Shadow DOM and styling

- All card HTML lives inside `this.shadowRoot` (via `attachShadow({ mode: "open" })`)
- Styles are scoped to the shadow DOM — use `<style>` tags inside the shadow root
- HA theme CSS variables (e.g., `var(--primary-color)`, `var(--card-background-color)`) are inherited into shadow DOM and should be used for consistent theming
- External stylesheets and global CSS do not penetrate the shadow boundary

## 11) Card structure

Every custom card must:

1. Extend `HTMLElement`
2. Define `setConfig(config)` — called once with card YAML config
3. Define `set hass(hass)` — called on every HA state change; use to read sensor data and trigger renders
4. Register with `customElements.define("<card-name>", CardClass)`
5. Optionally define `getCardSize()` — returns estimated card height in grid units

### Minimal skeleton

```javascript
class MyCard extends HTMLElement {
  setConfig(config) {
    this._config = config;
    if (!this.shadowRoot) {
      this.attachShadow({ mode: "open" });
    }
  }

  set hass(hass) {
    this._hass = hass;
    this._render();
  }

  _render() {
    // Build shadow DOM HTML
  }

  getCardSize() { return 3; }
}

customElements.define("my-card", MyCard);
```

## 11) Testing across devices

When testing custom cards, verify on all three target platforms:

| Feature | Desktop | iOS App | Android / UniFi |
|---|---|---|---|
| Button taps | click | touch→click | touch→click |
| Text input focus + keyboard | click | tap | tap (fragile) |
| Select/dropdown | click | tap | tap (fragile) |
| Date picker | click | tap | tap (fragile) |
| Scroll without triggering actions | mouse wheel | swipe | swipe |
| Relay service calls | always works | works if non-admin safe | works if non-admin safe |

Items marked "fragile" on Android/UniFi are sensitive to `preventDefault()` — follow §2 strictly.

Additional media-card checks:

| Feature | Desktop | iOS App | Android / UniFi |
|---|---|---|---|
| Same image stays visible across pause/state changes | verify | critical | verify |
| Next image appears without black frame | verify | critical | verify |
| Portrait and landscape images preserve card height | verify | verify | verify |
| Swipe navigation updates both text and image | verify | critical | verify |
